The IPR is essentially the inverse of the number of sites at which the wavefunction has a non-zero
amplitude. Furthermore this number is weighted by how large the probability density on this site is.

It is the wavefunction to the power of 4, evaluated at every point in the crystal. Or the square of the
probability density integrated over the whole crystal.

It has to be the square of this because if it was left as just the probability density it would be 1 always.
When you square the density it emphasises how much wavefunction is at a given site, and if there's a small amount,
this will be accounted for in the summation, as the square will reduce its amplitude.
